  "text": "This morning in Marseille, I came to express the Nation's solidarity with all those who were struck by the violence of October 17.\n\nI met with the mayor, the elected officials of the territory, the prefectural authorities, shopkeepers, residents, and the internal security forces.\n\nWe have drawn lessons from October 17. This Saturday, October 24, the right to demonstrate was preserved, and republican order was maintained. I warmly thank our law enforcement forces for their commitment alongside the elected officials.\n\nOur determination is unwavering to ensure that calm endures.\n\nWe will maintain the necessary security measures and work to establish permanent demonstration routes outside the city center, to reconcile the freedom to demonstrate, the safety of residents, and the resumption of economic activity.\n\nOur priority: preserve calm, allow shops to reopen, and let life fully resume its course.\n\nTo support shopkeepers who suffered looting or were forced to close, various forms of aid, including a support fund, have been put in place, in partnership with local authorities and the private sector. We are working with insurers and banks to accelerate compensation, help finance new orders, and support those who have experienced revenue losses.\n\nBehind every business, there are women and men who work, jobs, families, and years of effort. This is the economic life of our country. We must protect it and enable them to resume their activities.\n\nMarseille needs it. Our fellow citizens need it. The State is by their side and will remain so.\n\nI also know the angers that are being expressed. The high cost of living, the concerns of our high school and college students about their future: these are legitimate preoccupations that call for concrete responses. The Government will announce measures as early as this afternoon.\n\nBut no anger can justify violence, looting, or attacks on our institutions. Demonstrating is a right. Destroying never will be.\n\nSix months before the presidential election, our country needs a democratic debate, not a spiral of violence. Disagreements must be able to be expressed, proposals confronted, and the French people freely choose their future.\n\nWe will rise to the challenge of this collective responsibility: guaranteeing republican order, enabling democratic debate, and preserving what unites us.\n\nMarseillais, shopkeepers, elected officials, security forces: you can count on the Government and on my commitment. We will be there.",
